        <p class="section-label">
          A Life of Faith and Service
        </p>

        <h2>
          The Missionary Who Made Mangalore His Home
        </h2>

        <p class="lead">
          In November 1910, the city of Mangalore,
          India, mourned the death of a German-born
          Jesuit priest whose charitable work had
          become part of the community's life.
        </p>

        <p>
          Father Augustus Müller had spent more
          than three decades serving the people
          of South Canara. What began as a small
          homeopathic dispensary eventually
          developed into a network of charitable
          healthcare institutions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Among his most enduring undertakings
          was St. Joseph's Leprosy Hospital,
          established to care for people who
          were frequently marginalized and neglected.
        </p>

        <p>
          His obituary, published shortly after
          his death, described a man whose name
          had become familiar throughout the
          region and whose passing left a
          profound absence in the institutions
          he founded.
        </p>

        <p>
          Yet Father Müller's story began far
          from India. His journey would take him
          from Germany to the United States,
          then to France, and ultimately to
          the missionary fields of southern India.
        </p>

        <p>
          Along the way, illness, religious
          vocation, and an interest in homeopathy
          would shape the direction of his life.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


