        </p>

        <h2>
          From Westphalia to the Society of Jesus
        </h2>

        <p>
          Father Müller was born
          <strong>Friedrich August Müller</strong>
          on March 13, 1841, in Westphalia,
          Germany.
        </p>

        <p>
          His baptismal record, preserved in
          the parish registers of St. Marien
          in Hagen, provides an important
          connection to his earliest years.
        </p>

        <p>
          The entry records his baptism on
          March 21, 1841, and identifies
          his parents as
          <strong>
            Johann Müller and Catharina Kersting.
          </strong>
        </p>

        <figure class="document-figure">

          <a
            href={images.baptism}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            <img
              src={images.baptism}
              alt="1841 baptismal register containing the entry for Friedrich August Müller"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </a>

          <figcaption>
            <strong>
              The Baptism of Friedrich August Müller
            </strong>

            <span>
              St. Marien, Hagen, 1841.
              Entry no. 33 records his birth
              and baptism.
              Archdiocesan Archives of Paderborn,
              via Matricula Online.
            </span>

            <a
              href={sources.baptism}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              View Original Register ↗
            </a>
          </figcaption>

        </figure>

        <p>
          In 1861, at the age of twenty,
          Müller traveled to New York and
          entered the Society of Jesus
          on September 24.
        </p>

        <p>
          According to his obituary,
          following his novitiate he spent
          six years at St. John's College
          in Fordham, New York, and another
          year at St. Mary's College
          in Montreal.
        </p>

        <p>
          He subsequently continued his
          religious formation at Woodstock
          College in Maryland, where he
          studied philosophy and theology.
        </p>

        <p>
          It was during these years that
          serious health difficulties
          interrupted his studies and
          changed the course of his life.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         HOMEOPATHY
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="article-section section-parchment">

      <div class="article-inner">

        <p class="section-label">
          An Unexpected Turning Point
        </p>

        <h2>
          His Introduction to Homeopathy
        </h2>

        <p>
          Father Müller's obituary provides
          a detailed account of his introduction
          to homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p>
          After several years of study at
          Woodstock, his health deteriorated
          to the point that his physicians
          recommended a change of environment.
          He was sent to France.
        </p>

        <p>
          The obituary explains that Müller
          had already encountered homeopathy
          while living in America, having
          received treatment from
          <strong>Dr. McManus of Baltimore.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          In France, after conventional
          treatments reportedly failed to
          improve his condition, a friend
          encouraged him to pursue
          homeopathic treatment.
        </p>

        <p>
          He subsequently consulted
          <strong>Dr. Espanet of Paris.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          The obituary attributes Müller's
          recovery to this period of treatment,
          describing an improvement that
          profoundly influenced his understanding
          of homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p class="historical-note">
          This account reflects how his
          contemporaries understood his
          recovery. It is not independent
          clinical evidence establishing
          the effectiveness of the treatment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The experience nevertheless appears
          to have been decisive in shaping
          his future interests.
        </p>

        <p>
          With permission from his Jesuit
          superiors and under Dr. Espanet's
          guidance, Müller began studying
          homeopathy.
        </p>

        <p>
          His obituary records that he
          devoted part of each day to this
          study and began treating fellow
          members of his religious community.
        </p>

        <p>
          For Müller, the study of medicine
          would become closely connected
          with his desire to serve as
          a missionary.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         MANGALORE / BANYAN TREE
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="article-section">

      <div class="article-inner">

        <p class="section-label">
          Mangalore, India • 1878
        </p>

        <h2>
          Beneath the Banyan Tree
        </h2>

        <p>
          On
          <strong>December 31, 1878,</strong>
          Father Augustus Müller arrived
          in Mangalore, India, as part of
          an early group of Jesuit missionaries
          assigned to the region.
        </p>

        <p>
          After a period of parish ministry,
          he joined the teaching staff of
          the newly established St. Aloysius
          College, where he taught French
          and mathematics.
        </p>

        <p>
          Although his primary responsibilities
          were those of a Jesuit priest and
          educator, Müller had brought something
          unusual with him from Europe:
          a small chest of homeopathic medicines
          obtained from the firm of
          Catellan in Paris.
        </p>

        <div class="story-highlight">

          <span class="highlight-ornament">
            ✦
          </span>

          <p>
            It was beneath the shade of
            a banyan tree that the beginnings
            of his future charitable institutions
            took shape.
          </p>

        </div>

        <p>
          According to institutional histories,
          he began offering homeopathic
          medicines to students and others
          seeking his assistance.
        </p>

        <p>
          As word spread, parents and members
          of the surrounding community
          also came to him.
        </p>

        <p>
          What began as a simple act of
          service gradually grew beyond
          the confines of the college.
        </p>

        <p>
          The banyan tree became a lasting
          symbol of Father Müller's charitable
          mission—a reminder that the
          institutions bearing his name
          began not with grand buildings
          or abundant resources, but with
          a priest responding to the needs
          of those around him.
        </p>

        <p>
          In <strong>1880</strong>,
          the growing demand led to the
          establishment of the
          <strong>
            Homoeopathic Poor Dispensary.
          </strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Yet for Father Müller,
          this work represented more than
          an interest in medicine.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =====================================================
         FAITH / VOCATION
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="faith-section">

      <div class="faith-inner">

        <p class="section-label">
          Faith in Action
        </p>

        <h2>
          A Calling to Serve
        </h2>

        <p class="faith-intro">
          Father Müller's religious vocation
          and his desire to care for the
          suffering became deeply intertwined.
        </p>

        <blockquote>

          <p>
            “Believing that he had been
            chosen by God to use Homeopathy
            to care for the sick,
            Fr Muller started his mission.”
          </p>

          <cite>
            Father Muller Charitable Institutions
            <span>2017 institutional publication</span>
          </cite>

        </blockquote>

        <p>
          These words, written by the
          institution rather than Father
          Müller himself, preserve how
          his successors understood the
          spiritual motivation behind
          his work.
        </p>

        <p>
          His vocation as a Jesuit priest
          and his desire to care for the
          suffering were not separate
          pursuits. They became two
          expressions of the same calling.
        </p>

